  "text_md": "TROEN.\n\nPeny rydys iroen be tioen, Ovyr all in ylke a toen. On land and eke on flode. Reliq.\n\nspinner. Forby\n\n(2) of roasted meat, which before the whole can be dressed becomes tough and dry, like a trip overkept, or the leather used by the old woman. Forby. TRISE. To pull up.\n\nAntiq. ii. 110. TROFELYTE. Ornamented with knots. Gaw. TROGH. A tree.\n\nTRISE. To pull up.\n\nTROGHTE. Belief? The thryde es\n\nTRISTE. (1)\n\nfor-thy that we have Alle o troghte that sal us save. MS. Harl. 2260,\n\nbe prowde and eke of port For frys^eof lady and eke of lord. M8. Cott. Claud. A.\n\nMS. Harl. 2260, f. 21 _\n\nMS. Cott. Claud. A.li.f. 140.\n\nTROIFLARDES. Triflers ; idlers. TROITE. The cuttle-fish ? \" Sepia, Anglice a troite,\" Nominale MS. TROJAN. A boon companion a person who\n\n(2) To trust. I was\n\nwel ye wist, prison\n\nTo helpe of you ne myght I triste: MS. Addit. 11305, f. 90.\n\n; is fond of liquor. A cant term. According to some, a thief was so called ; but it was applied somewhat indiscriminately. A rough manly boy is now termed \" a fine Trojan.\" Grose has trusty Trojan, a true friend.\n\nMy lorde, when he went to the see, For tryste he toke me to the. specyalle\n\n(3) A post or station in hunting:\n\nhas trusty Trojan, 9 true friend.\n\nI se huntynge, I se hornes blow,\n\nHoundes renne, the dere drawe adowne,\n\nTROKE. (1 To barter to truck. North.\n\nAnd atte her triste bowes arow, set\n\n(2) To fall short.\n\nNow in August this Iusti fressh cesone\n\nHe mone stond faste thereby,\n\nOr ellys hys schote wolle troke.\n\nA trestle, or support. A windlass.\n\nMS. Porkington 10, f. 58\n\nA windlass:\n\nTROKES: Square pieces of wood at the tops of masts to put the fag-staffs in.\n\nA cattle-market North_\n\nTRISTER. See Triste (3)\n\nTROLL. To trundle To troll the bowl, to pass the vessel about in drinking.\n\nTRISTESCE. Sadness.\n\nSave only that I crye aud bidde, am in tristesce alle amidde.\n\nTROLLEN To draw ; to A-s)\n\nthe French in the 16 th century, now known under the name of trunks, q. v. Brand quotes a curious account of this game, from which it appears to have formed a favorite indoor amusement with the lady fashionables at Buxton about the year 1572, and to have been somewhat like the modern game of bagatelle. There is an allusion to it in the Winter's Tale, iv. 2. TROLLOP. A slattern. Far. dial.\n\nf. 126. 134 ,\n\nTRISTILY. Safely ; securely. \" tristily had tretyd,\" Morte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 57. TRISTIVE. Sad. {Lat.)\n\nTRISTIVE. Sad. (Lat.)\n\nTRISTUR. The same as Triste (3).\n\nTRIUJPH. (1) ^ public show;\n\nof (2) A trump at   cards. The game trump was also so called.\n\nTRIVANT. A truant a loiterer.\n\nTROLLOP. (1) A slattern: Var. dial.\n\nTRIVET. Right A common phrase.\n\n(2) A string of horses. Linc.\n\nTÉOLLOPISII. Filtby ; dirty. South.\n\nTRIVIGANT. Termagant. (Ital)\n\nTROLLY. A low heavy cart.\n\nTRIWEDE Honesty. Hearne.\n\nTROLLYBAGS. Tripe. Var. dial.\n\nTROACHER A dealer in smuggled goods.\n\nTROLLY-LOLLY. Coarse lace.\n\nA foolish fellow . Exm.\n\nTROLUBBER. and ditcher. Devon. hedger\n\nTRO AT. To bellow, said TROCHE. To branch. (A.-N.)\n\nTROME. Band, or company. (A-s)\n\nTROCIIE . To branch. (A.-N)\n\nTROMPE. (1) A trumpet. (A.-N)\n\nTROCHES. Were thus made:\n\n(2) A shin, or shank.\n\nwood of aloes eight ounces, styrax calamite three ounces, musk half a dram, orrice two ounces, sugar-candy three pound • powder them, and with rose-water make troches. Cosmeticks, 1660. p. 138. TROCHINGS. The cluster of small branches\n\nTROMPOUR A trumpetcr.\n\nTRONAGE. A toll for the weighing of wool in the market. Coles .\n\nTRONCHON. A fragment.\n\nTROCIIINGS. The cluster of small branches at the of a stag's horn. top\n\nTRONCIION. fragment. (A-N)\n\nTROD. A footpath. Linc. Ran from trod to trod,' Du Bartas; P. 360.\n\nHe set the hed of the bore. MS. Cantab. Ff.\n\nTRODE. Track ; (A-s) path.\n\nii. 38, f. 100. TRONE. (1) A throne. {A.-N.) It is the verb, to enthrone, in this example. And ther soulys to hevyn here.\n\nYf thou ever trowyde ore uudyrstode gude\n\nThat thl wytt ore thl\n\nAnd ther soulys to hevyn bere,\n\nCommys of thiselfe and nozte of Gode, That e8 grett pryde and fals trode\n\nBcfore God tronyd they\n\nwere\n\nMS:\n\nBowes, P.lô.\n\n(2) A ridge of mown WVest hay.\n\n# TRODUS. Steps.\n\n(3 A post, or log of\n\nwher he was a-go, Ne of his trodus no sygne ther nasse. Ch)vn. Vitodun,\n\nTRONES. steelyard. Norlh.\n\nTROYSOUN.\n\n",
